## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design offline-first features, idempotency, and conflict handling for mobile apps. Challenges include eventual consistency, sync retries, and safe canary rollout.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first architecture
- Idempotent API design and deduplication
- Conflict resolution with timestamped records
- Data model versioning and canary controls
- Local testing strategies for offline/sync workflows

## Code Example
```javascript
function mergeScore(local, incoming) {
  // last write wins by client_timestamp per user
  if (incoming.client_timestamp > local.client_timestamp) {
    return { ...local, ...incoming };
  }
  return local;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test offline queue size and eviction policy?
- How would you handle partial failures during batch sync?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes a candidate's ability to design a robust, privacy-preserving streaming system with practical API design, data modeling, and real-world constraints (late arrivals, auditing, fault tolerance).

## Key Concepts

- Secure aggregation in streaming systems
- Tamper-evident append-only logging
- Late-arrival handling and fault tolerance
- Differential privacy budgeting in continuous streams
- API design for edge-device contributions

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: verify and accumulate a batch of shares securely
function processBatch(batch){
  const maskedSum = batch.shares.reduce((a,s)=>a + s.maskedValue,0);
  // server-side de-masking happens with stored keys
  return unmask(maskedSum);
}
```

